WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.210
Hi, my name is Helen Zhou,
and the goal of my project

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:05.340
is to predict
antibiotic resistance.

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:07.540
Antibiotic resistance
is a major problem.

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:10.500
It's responsible for 23,000
annual deaths in the United

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.380
States alone, and 700,000
annual deaths worldwide.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.650
One of the leading causes
of antibiotic resistance

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:20.520
is the overprescription of
overly broad antibiotics.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.510
This motivates the
focus of our project,

00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:25.280
which is in the empiric
antibiotic treatment setting.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:27.780
In this setting, a patient
comes in with an infection

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:29.940
and the doctor needs to
make an immediate treatment

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:32.910
decision before the
patient is sent home.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:35.160
They have to do this using
only the information that's

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.620
available to them at
the time, and they

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.050
want to prescribe something
that is both effective

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:41.700
and doesn't have too
many negative side

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:44.617
effects for both the
individual and the population.

00:00:44.617 --> 00:00:46.950
However, they're missing a
critical piece of information

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:49.260
at this point in time,
and that's precisely what

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.334
is the patient resistant to.

00:00:51.334 --> 00:00:52.750
And so since this
is so important,

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:55.410
we want to predict resistance
at an individual level

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:56.940
at the point of care.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:59.310
We do this by using
machine learning models

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:01.450
to combine information
from many modalities,

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:03.810
such as natural language
clinician's notes, diagnoses,

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:06.250
procedures, lab values.

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:09.600
And we do this for 275,000
patients from the Brigham

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.320
and MGH across
more than 17 years.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:16.184
As far as we know, this is at a
larger scale and broader scope

00:01:16.184 --> 00:01:18.600
than previous work, and we're
able to do these predictions

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.400
quite well.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.800
So in the future,
we hope that we'll

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:24.990
be able to provide these
accurate predictions that

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:27.120
are also timely to
doctors so that they

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:31.010
can provide the best care that
they can for their patients.

